Z||000000||pHoOl||01-24-2001||07:56 PM||||I've heard a lot of you say that you're just going to build your own dash.  Not that i'd be doing it anytime soon, but maybe with the next fiero, i'm somewhat thinking of trying to build a custom one.  What materials do you make it out of?  I would have no clue as to where to start.  I thought maybe you start with wood, then cover it with vinal or something, but i thought that might be very heavy.<P>Give me hints!!!<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierosound||01-24-2001||08:09 PM||||Building a custom dash coule be major work, depending on what you want to do.  The site below shows what's involved in a completely redesigned dash.<P> <A HREF="http://www.perigee.net/~lunapark/pages/dash.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.perigee.net/~lunapark/pages/dash.html</A> ||||reg||10
Z||000002||GeeDee86GT||01-26-2001||12:57 PM||||The latest issue of 'Street Rod Builder' has a multi page article of making a dash/console from fiberglass.  Gives a good idea of what your up against. Bear in mind that in our cars a lot of heavy gear has to be supported, therefore lots of wood or metal bracing. Stop giving me more ideas of things to do....<BR>Gary<BR>2 86 SE's<BR>84 base||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||01-26-2001||04:47 PM||||WOOHOO!!!! I just bought that mag this afternoon, for that article!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||14
Z||000004||rogergarrison||01-27-2001||08:06 AM||||thin sheet metal for curves like over instr cluster, I use 1/8 Luan wall paneling for most, aluminum angle to put pieces together. Headliner materiel with foam makes great padding, thencover with vinyl or leather. I also make a lot of the pieces from cardboard too first for trial fitting, ez to trim and fit. then use it for pattern.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 01-27-2001).]||||reg||1
